.TH EUCLID-CLI-ESM-EXISTS-BUCKET 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-esm-exists-bucket \- whether a bucket exists, as an exit code for a script
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli esm exists-bucket
.BR \-\-bucket | \-b
.I NAME
.SH DESCRIPTION
Answers whether a bucket exists. Written to be branched on rather than
read: a shell treats a successful exit as true, so the answer is the exit
status.
.PP
.B "true"
or
.B "false"
also goes to standard output, and nothing else does \(em no JSON, no
metadata, nothing to cut out of the way with
.BR sed (1).
So the same command serves
.B if
and
.BR "$(\|.\|.\|.\|)" .
.PP
The question has three answers, not two. Alongside "yes" and "no" there
is "could not tell": an expired session, an unreachable gateway, a
refused permission. Those exit
.BR 2 ,
so a script never reads an outage as an absent bucket and goes on to
create one that was already there, or skip a step that mattered. A script
that only branches on
.B if
still gets the safe outcome, because
.B 2
is not success either and the true branch is not taken on an answer
nobody actually got.
.PP
Asks about the bucket, not about anything in it. A bucket that exists
and holds no objects is still there, and this says so.
.PP
Needs
.IR esm:get-bucket-ern ,
because that is the call it makes. A caller without it gets
.B 2
rather than a wrong answer.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bucket " " \fINAME\fR
Name of the bucket to look for. Required. A missing
.B \-\-bucket
is a broken command line rather than an absent bucket, and exits
.BR 2 .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The bucket exists.
.B "true"
is written to standard output.
.TP
.B 1
The bucket does not exist.
.B "false"
is written to standard output.
.TP
.B 2
The question could not be answered: no
.BR \-\-bucket ,
an unreachable or unauthenticated gateway, a refused permission, or any
other error. The reason is written to standard error and standard output
is left empty.
.SH EXAMPLES
Act only if it is not already there:
.RS
.nf
if ! euclid\-cli esm exists\-bucket \-b apps > /dev/null; then
    echo "creating apps"
fi
.fi
.RE
.PP
Tell the three answers apart, which any script that acts on absence
should do:
.RS
.nf
euclid\-cli esm exists\-bucket \-b apps > /dev/null
case $? in
    0) echo "already there" ;;
    1) echo "absent" ;;
    2) echo "could not tell \(em leaving it alone" >&2; exit 1 ;;
esac
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-get-bucket-ern (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-create-bucket (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-get-bucket (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-exists-queue (1)
